course: world
lesson: 27
title: Habitats and adaptations
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A habitat is the place where a living thing lives and finds food, water, shelter and a mate: a desert, the Arctic, the ocean or a forest.
explain: An adaptation is a feature of the body or behaviour that helps a living thing survive in its habitat; adaptations grow over very many generations (parents, then their young, then their young's young, and so on), not during one animal's life.
explain: A camel stores fat (not water) in its hump, can go many days without drinking, has wide feet for soft sand and can close its nostrils against blowing sand; a cactus stores water in its thick stem and has spines instead of leaves to lose less water.
explain: A polar bear has thick fat, dense fur and black skin under its pale fur; it has small ears and wide feet for walking on snow. A fish has gills, fins and a smooth shape for water.
explain: In a rainforest, monkeys have gripping hands and tails for the trees, and many animals have colours that help them hide (camouflage) or that warn they are poisonous.
fact: A habitat is the natural home of a living thing.
fact: An adaptation is a feature that helps a living thing survive in its habitat.
fact: Adaptations develop over many generations, not in one animal's life.
fact: A camel's hump stores fat, not water.
fact: Cacti store water in thick stems and have spines instead of broad leaves.
fact: Spines lose much less water than leaves and also protect the cactus from animals.
fact: Polar bears have a thick layer of fat and dense fur to keep warm.
fact: Many Arctic animals have small ears, which lose less heat.
fact: Many desert animals, like the fennec fox, have big ears that help them lose heat.
fact: Many desert animals sleep in burrows by day and come out in the cool night.
fact: Fish have gills to take oxygen from water and a smooth shape to swim easily.
fact: Camouflage is colouring that helps an animal blend in with its surroundings.
exercise: What is a habitat? | The place where a living thing lives | The food an animal eats | The way an animal moves | The group an animal belongs to
exercise: What is an adaptation? | A feature that helps a living thing survive | A change an animal makes just by wishing hard | A new home an animal moves into | A type of food in a habitat
exercise: What does a camel store in its hump? | Fat | Water | Air | Milk
exercise: Why does a cactus have spines instead of broad leaves? | To lose less water and keep animals off | To catch more sunlight on hot days | To store more water in the spines | To help the wind carry its seeds
exercise: Where does a cactus store water? | In its thick stem | In its spines | In its flowers | In the sand around it
exercise: What keeps a polar bear warm? | Thick fat and dense fur | Long legs and big ears | Thin skin and short fur | A hump of stored water
exercise: Why do many Arctic animals have small ears? | Small ears lose less heat | Small ears hear better in snow | Small ears are easier to clean | Small ears scare away enemies
exercise: Why does the desert fennec fox have very large ears? | They help it lose heat | They keep sand out of its eyes | They store water for dry days | They help it dig burrows faster
exercise: Why do many desert animals come out at night? | It is cooler then | There is more food in the dark | They are afraid of the Sun | They cannot see in daylight
exercise: What helps a fish live in water? | Gills, fins and a smooth shape | Lungs, fur and wide feet | Feathers, beak and wings | Spines, a hump and big ears
exercise: What is camouflage? | Colours that help an animal blend in | A thick coat that keeps an animal warm | A long journey to a warm place | A deep sleep through the winter
exercise: How do adaptations appear? | Slowly over many generations | In one animal during its life | Overnight when the weather changes | When an animal moves to a new place
exercise: Why do monkeys in a rainforest have gripping hands and tails? | To climb and hold on in the trees | To dig for roots in the soil | To swim across wide rivers | To keep warm on cold nights
exercise: Why do some poisonous frogs have bright colours? | To warn predators they are poisonous | To hide among the green leaves | To attract insects to eat | To stay warm in the shade
test: An animal has white fur, small ears, thick fat and wide furry feet. Where does it most likely live? | In the Arctic | In a hot desert | In a rainforest | In the deep ocean
test: A plant has a fat stem, no leaves, a waxy skin and long shallow roots. Where does it most likely grow? | In a desert | In a pond | In the Arctic sea | On a dark forest floor
test: A polar bear is moved to a hot desert. What would its fur and fat do there? | Make it overheat | Help it find water | Keep it cool | Hide it on the sand
test: A boy says a giraffe got a long neck by stretching to reach leaves all its life. What is right? | Long necks developed over many generations | He is right, stretching all its life made it longer | Giraffes were born with short necks | Necks grow when animals eat a lot
test: A moth is brown and speckled and rests on tree bark. How does this help it? | Birds find it hard to see | It keeps the moth warm | It makes the moth taste bad | It helps the moth find food
test: A desert lizard hides under a rock at midday. Why? | To keep cool out of the hot sun | To find some snow to drink | To lay eggs in the shade only | To warm up after the cold night
test: Which is a myth about camels? | Their humps are full of water | They can go days without drinking | They have wide feet for sand | They can close their nostrils
test: A new fish is found with a flat body, eyes on top and sandy colours. Where does it most likely live? | Lying on the sandy sea floor | Swimming fast in open water | Hiding among red corals | Living in a fresh mountain stream
test: Why could a cactus not survive in a dark, wet forest well? | It is adapted to bright light and dry soil | It needs to eat forest animals | It cannot make any seeds there | Rain would wash its spines off
test: why's a polar bear got so much fat | To keep warm in the cold | To store water for the summer | To float better in hot seas | To make it run faster
test: why's a fennec fox got huge ears | To help it lose heat in the hot desert | To keep its head warm at night | To store water inside them | To dig its burrow faster
test: my cousin says a camel's hump is full of water. true? | No, it stores fat | Yes, it's a big water tank | Yes, and it's full of milk too | No, it's full of air to float
test: Leo reckons a giraffe got a long neck by stretching it all its life. right? | No, adaptations develop over many generations | Yes, each giraffe stretches its own neck longer | Yes, and its babies are born with a stretched neck | No, its neck grows only when it eats leaves
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term adaptation? | A feature that assists a living thing to survive in its habitat | A change that an animal makes deliberately within the course of its lifetime | The home of a living thing | A journey to a more suitable habitat
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the hump of the camel. | It stores fat | It stores water | It stores air | It stores undigested food
exam: Identify the principal reason for which many desert animals possess large ears. | The ears assist in the loss of bodily heat | The ears retain bodily heat | The ears store water | The ears shield the eyes from the Sun
exam: Identify the principal advantage conferred upon a cactus by its spines in place of broad leaves. | A reduced loss of water and protection from animals | An increased capacity to manufacture food in the sunlight | An increased absorption of rainfall | An attraction of pollinating insects
